NUCLEO-F303RE
=============

An STM32F303RE: a Cortex-M4 with 512K of flash, 64K of SRAM and 16K of
core coupled memory, and a floating point extension that is single
precision only.

The kernel now fits in the SRAM.  It still cannot run Limbo, and the
reason is no longer the link.

What was measured
-----------------

Linked with the configuration beside this file, which is the smallest
one that still links -- no root file system, no Limbo, no ip, no ssl, no
capabilities, no profiling:

	        was      is
	text	259416	279376	  flash is 524288, so this fits
	data	 50368	 24704
	bss	 38560	 24392
		------	------
		 88928	 49096	  RAM is 81920, so this now fits

The 39832 came from four changes, none of them to this directory: the
Dis tracker's and the pool history's tables are allocated on demand
rather than reserved (os/inferno/port/{alloc,heap}.c), the console log
is sized per board by KMESGSIZE, and the C string literals are linked
into the text segment by tl -t instead of being copied into RAM at boot.
See doc/xip.ms.

What is left is the pool floor: roughly 78K of kernel allocations before
anything is loaded, of which the largest items are the Proc array's
kernel stacks, the Dis type descriptors and addprog.  That is still more
than this board's whole SRAM, so a Limbo module has nowhere to live.
The floor is portable code and is measured in doc/xip.ms; it is not a
board problem and it is not fixed here.

The second wall
---------------

The Cortex-M4 has FPv4-SP, which has no double precision instructions,
and utils/tl/thumb.c gives every F register a D register -- the way the
FPA it replaces held one value whatever the memory format was.  Linking
with

	tl -M -F ...

names every function that would fault: the Dis interpreter's real
arithmetic, its conversions, and everything in libmath.

This is now handled the way os/inferno/port/fpiarm.c handled the FPA on
ARM: os/inferno/armv7m/fpiarmv7m.c decodes the faulting double
precision instruction out of the UsageFault and emulates it with
os/inferno/port/fpi.c.  It is in this board's mkfile.  On the mps2
Cortex-M4 bench, where it can be run, fptest(1) passes 25 of 25.  On
this board it has never been run, because nothing here has.

What is here anyway
-------------------

The board itself: the clock tree taken to 64MHz off the internal
oscillator with the flash wait states and the APB1 divider that go with
it, and the USART the ST-LINK's virtual serial port is wired to on PA2
and PA3.  None of it has been run.  It is written from RM0316 and the
NUCLEO-64 schematic, and it is the part that a Cortex-M4 with more
memory -- an STM32F4 or F7 -- would want, since those differ from this
in their memory and their peripherals and not in their core.

What would have to change
-------------------------

The kernel pool floor, which is portable work, or a board with more
RAM.  Not a change to this directory.
